==English==
{{wikipedia|dab=induction}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|induction|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|inductiō|lang=la}}, from {{term|indūcō||I lead|lang=la}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ɪnˈdʌkʃən/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ʌkʃən|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# An act of [[induct]]ing.
#* Beaumont and Fletcher
#*: I know not you; nor am I well pleased to make this time, as the affair now stands, the '''induction''' of your acquaintance.
#* Shakespeare
#*: These promises are fair, the parties sure, / And our '''induction''' full of prosperous hope.
## A [[formal]] [[ceremony]] in which a person is appointed to an [[office]] or into [[military]] [[service]].
# An act of [[induce|inducing]].
#* {{quote-book|passage=One of the first examples of the immunogenicity of recombinantly derived antibodies was with murine anti-CD3 monoclonal antibody (OKT3) used in the '''induction''' of immunosupression after organ transplantation.|page=699|title=Modern Pharmaceutics|edition=4th|author=Gilbert S. Banker & Christopher T. Rhodes|publisher=Informa Health Care|year=2002|isbn=0824706749}}
## {{context|physics|lang=en}} [[generation|Generation]] of an [[electric]] [[current]] by a [[varying]] [[magnetic]] [[field]].
## {{context|logic|lang=en}} [[derivation|Derivation]] of [[general]] [[principle]]s from [[specific]] [[instance]]s.
## {{context|mathematics|lang=en}} A method of [[proof]] of a [[theorem]] by first proving it for a specific case (often an [[integer]]; usually 0 or 1) and showing that, if it is true for one case then it must be true for the next.
## {{context|theater|lang=en}} Use of rumors to twist and [[complicate]] the plot of a play or to [[narrate]] in a way that does not have to state truth nor fact within the play.
## {{context|biology|lang=en}} In [[developmental]] [[biology]], the development of a feature from part of a formerly [[homogenous]] field of cells in response to a [[morphogen]] whose source determines the feature's position and extent.
# {{lb|en|medicine}} The process of [[inducing]] the birth process.
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} An [[introduction]].
#* Massinger
#*: This is but an '''induction''': I will daw / The curtains of the tragedy hereafter.

